What is the value of `x` after this code runs?

```java
int a = 5;
int b = 8;
boolean x = (a != b);
```

What is the value of `result`?

```java
int x = 4;
boolean result = (x * 2 >= 8) == (x < 10);
```

What is printed?

```java
boolean flag = false;
System.out.println(!flag);
```

What is printed?

```java
int x = 10;
if (x > 5) {
    System.out.println("big");
}
```

What is printed?

```java
int x = 3;
if (x > 5) {
    System.out.println("big");
}
System.out.println("done");
```

What is printed?

```java
int x = 2;
if (x > 5)
    System.out.println("A");
    System.out.println("B");
```

What is printed?

```java
int score = 75;
if (score >= 60) {
    System.out.println("pass");
}
if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A grade");
}
```

What is printed?

```java
int x = 4;
if (x % 2 == 0) {
    System.out.println("even");
} else {
    System.out.println("odd");
}
```

What is printed?

```java
int score = 85;
if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("B");
} else {
    System.out.println("C");
}
```

What is printed?

```java
int x = 50;
if (x > 10) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else if (x > 30) {
    System.out.println("B");
} else if (x > 5) {
    System.out.println("C");
}
```

What is printed?

```java
int x = 7;
if (x < 0) {
    System.out.println("negative");
} else if (x == 0) {
    System.out.println("zero");
} else {
    System.out.println("positive");
}
```

What is printed?

```java
boolean isMember = true;
double price = 20.0;
if (isMember) {
    price = price * 0.9;
}
System.out.println(price);
```

What is the value of this expression?

```java
int x = 5;
boolean result = (x > 0) && (x < 10);
```

What is the value of this expression?

```java
int age = 15;
boolean canRide = (age >= 18) || (age >= 12);
```

What is the value of this expression?

```java
int x = 6;
int y = 2;
boolean result = !(x > 5 && y > 5);
```

What is printed?

```java
int x = 0;
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};
if (x < arr.length && arr[x] == 1) {
    System.out.println("match");
} else {
    System.out.println("no match");
}
```

Java's `&&` uses short-circuit evaluation: the right side is only checked if the left side is true.

What is printed?

```java
String a = new String("cat");
String b = new String("cat");
System.out.println(a == b);
```

What is printed?

```java
String a = new String("cat");
String b = new String("cat");
System.out.println(a.equals(b));
```

What is printed?

```java
String a = "cat";
String b = "cat";
System.out.println(a == b);
```

String literals in Java are typically stored in a shared pool, so identical literals often refer to the same object -- unlike Strings created with `new`.

What is printed?

```java
String a = null;
System.out.println(a == null);
```

What is wrong with this code, given that `name` might be `null`?

```java
String name = null;
if (name.equals("Alex")) {
    System.out.println("found");
}
```

What is printed?

```java
int temp = 32;
if (temp <= 32) {
    System.out.println("freezing");
}
if (temp > 32) {
    System.out.println("not freezing");
}
```
